# W44-90 — paired interleaved A/B/C for PixelLossDispatch default-flip decision

## Provenance

- Date:                   2026-05-19 UTC
- Hostname:               lilith (water-cooled AMD Ryzen 9 7950X, 32 threads, 128 GB RAM)
- Parent commit:          e0178b55 (W44-89 honest-stop on main)
- Workspace:              ~/work/zen/jxl-encoder--w44-90-pixelloss-default-flip
- Encoder build:          release, features `std parallel butteraugli-loop`
- Target dir:             $HOME/work/zen/jxl-encoder-shared-target (per disk-fill mitigation)
- Decoder for metrics:    jxl-oxide linear-sRGB
- Butteraugli:            Rust `butteraugli_linear` (CLAUDE.md PNG-metadata-immune path)
- SSIM2:                  fast_ssim2 (sRGB u8 inputs after linear→sRGB conversion)

## Goal

W38-2 (`33011181`) shipped the `PixelLossDispatch` enum scaffolding
with `AlwaysOn` (default), `Auto` (mask1x1-median gate at 80), and
`AlwaysOff`. The original A/B was narrow (8 images, 144 cells).

This bench widens the corpus to 22 images × 5 distances × 3 dispatches
× 5 trials (interleaved, per W44-89 methodology) = 1650 encodes, to
decide whether `Auto` or `AlwaysOff` can become the default at e5
without RD regression on any cell.

## Acceptance gates

- Bytes Δ vs `AlwaysOn` baseline: ≤ +5 % on every cell
- Butteraugli Δ: ≤ +3 % on every cell
- SSIM2 Δ: ≥ -1.5 points on every cell
- Median perf win on smooth-photo cells (where `Auto` fires) ≥ 5 ms

A variant must pass ALL gates to be promoted to default.

## Method

- 3 variants run **back-to-back within each trial** (interleaved
  A/B/C order: AlwaysOn, AlwaysOff, Auto). 5 trials per cell.
  Variance recorded per cell (max - min / median).
- Per-trial raw rows also persisted to `.raw.tsv` for diagnostic /
  bisection.
- Each encode independently runs through the full pipeline (no
  shared encoder state between dispatches).

## Sweep grid

- Effort: e5 only (per W38-2 finding pixel_domain_loss matters most at e5)
- Distances: {0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 5.0}  (5 points covering low/mid/high d)
- Dispatches: {AlwaysOn, AlwaysOff, Auto}  (3)
- Trials: 5 (interleaved)
- Planned: 22 × 5 × 3 = 330 aggregate rows, 1650 encodes
- **Actual (truncated)**: 60 cells × 3 dispatches = 180 rows, 900 encodes.
  Photos (8) + mid-class (4) = 60 of 110 planned cells = 54 % coverage.
  Screenshot class (10 images, 50 cells) NOT included — bench was
  killed at the codec_wiki stage when the decision became conclusive
  from photos+mid alone (truncation justification in TRUNCATION below).

## TRUNCATION (partial bench, photos+mid only)

The bench was killed at row 181 / 330 (54 % cells) after both
acceptance gates were comfortably exhausted on the photo+mid subset:

- `auto`: **11 / 60 cells (18 %)** failed at least one acceptance gate
  on photos alone; **median Δms = -1.95 ms** on cells where Auto
  actually fires (5 ms target gate).
- `always_off`: 43 / 60 cells (72 %) failed (catastrophic).

No screenshot result can rescue `auto` to PASS because (a) gates are
per-cell (all cells must pass, screenshots only add cells), and (b)
screenshots typically lift `mask1x1 median` well above 80 so `Auto`
fires there ≈ `always_off`, which is already known to regress 0.6 ssim2
on screens at e5 per W38-2's narrow A/B. Adding 50 more cells with the
W38-2 ~0.6 ssim2 regression profile would increase the failure count,
not decrease it.

**Time budget**: bench ran ~70 minutes for photos+mid; codec_wiki
alone (~4 MP at e5 × 3 dispatches × 5 trials) was on track to take
20-30 minutes. Total bench would have run ~3.5 hours, far exceeding
the chunk's time-box. Decision-conclusive partial bench was the
appropriate call.

## Corpus (22 images)

### CID22-512 photos (8):
- 1025469.png, 1044329.png, 1189261.png, 1279330.png, 1418519.png,
  1420710.png, 1531677.png, 2389166.png

### CID22-512 mid-class (4):
- 3637739.png, 1624487.png, 1544947.png, 1475938.png

### GB82-SC screenshots (10):
- codec_wiki.png, imac_g3.png, imac_dark.png, terminal.png, windows.png,
  windows95.png, gmessages.png, imessage.png, graph.png, gui.png

## TSV columns (aggregate)

- `image`            : source filename
- `class`            : photo / mid / screen
- `effort`           : 5
- `distance`         : 0.5 / 1.0 / 2.0 / 3.0 / 5.0
- `dispatch`         : always_on / always_off / auto
- `width`, `height`  : input dimensions
- `bytes_med`        : 5-trial median bytes
- `butteraugli_med`  : 5-trial median butteraugli score
- `ssim2_med`        : 5-trial median SSIM2 score
- `encode_ms_med`    : 5-trial median encode wall-ms
- `encode_ms_min`    : minimum across trials
- `encode_ms_max`    : maximum across trials
- `encode_ms_p25`    : 25th percentile
- `encode_ms_p75`    : 75th percentile
- `variance_pct`     : 100 * (max - min) / median (acceptance gate from
                       W44-89 lesson: < 20 % is "stable measurement")

## Raw companion TSV

`.raw.tsv` companion has per-trial rows with columns:
- `image`, `class`, `effort`, `distance`, `dispatch`, `trial` (0..4),
  `width`, `height`, `bytes`, `butteraugli`, `ssim2`, `encode_ms`

## Reproducer

```bash
cd ~/work/zen/jxl-encoder
CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$HOME/work/zen/jxl-encoder-shared-target \
  cargo run -p jxl-encoder --release \
  --features 'std parallel butteraugli-loop' \
  --example w44_90_pixelloss_default_flip_ab -- \
  --out benchmarks/w44_90_pixelloss_default_flip_2026-05-19.tsv
```

## Analysis

```bash
python3 jxl-encoder/examples/w44_90_analyze.py \
  benchmarks/w44_90_pixelloss_default_flip_2026-05-19.tsv
```

The analyzer outputs per-variant gate tally + median Δ on bytes/bfly/
ssim2/ms across all cells, lists failing cells, and prints a final
ACCEPTANCE VERDICT line.

## Verdict

```
=== ACCEPTANCE VERDICT ===
  always_off: gates=FAIL (43 cells), perf=OK (median win +5.24ms on n=60 firing cells) — FAIL
  auto:       gates=FAIL (11 cells), perf=FAIL (median win +1.95ms on n=15 firing cells) — FAIL
```

**Both candidate variants fail the default-flip gates.** Default
stays `AlwaysOn`. No source change shipped.

### Failing cells under `auto`

The 11 cells where `Auto` regresses despite the mask1x1 > 80 gate:

| image            | d   | regression                               |
|---               |---  |---                                       |
| 1279330.png      | 0.5 | bytes +6.57 %                            |
| 1279330.png      | 2.0 | bytes +5.62 %                            |
| 1279330.png      | 3.0 | bfly +6.32 %                             |
| 1418519.png      | 0.5 | bytes +7.25 %, bfly +20.37 %             |
| 1418519.png      | 1.0 | bytes +6.34 %, bfly +25.11 %             |
| 1418519.png      | 2.0 | bfly +9.73 %                             |
| 1475938.png      | 0.5 | bfly +21.78 %                            |
| 1475938.png      | 1.0 | bfly +19.26 %                            |
| 1475938.png      | 2.0 | bfly +5.29 %                             |
| 1475938.png      | 3.0 | bfly +7.98 %                             |
| 1475938.png      | 5.0 | bfly +31.94 %                            |

**Diagnosis**: the `median(mask1x1) > 80` discriminator is too coarse.
It fires (correctly identifies smooth-LOOKING content) on
`1279330`/`1418519`/`1475938`, but on those particular smooth photos
the pixel-domain loss term is doing crucial AC-strategy-search work
that the coefficient-domain entropy estimate alone cannot recover.
Disabling it costs up to 25-32 % butteraugli.

### Diagnosis ranking — what would be required to rescue `Auto`

1. **A second-order discriminator** beyond `median(mask1x1) > 80`.
   The mid-class `1475938.png` was specifically added to this bench to
   probe this regime — and it fires the worst regression in the whole
   set (+31.94 % bfly at d=5.0). Per W22-1 / W23-2 the existing
   mask1x1 median has been audited as too coarse on multiple lifts.
   Likely candidates: `(palette_log2_size, flat_color_block_ratio)`
   pair from zenanalyze, or a stddev-of-mask1x1 term to detect mixed
   smooth-plus-detail content.

2. **Raise the gate**: `> 100` or `> 120` would exclude more cells
   from Auto firing, sacrificing perf savings on cells that currently
   DO benefit (smooth photo `1531677.png` at d=0.5 went from
   bfly +26.76% under `always_off` to +0 under `auto` — meaning Auto
   correctly skipped here). Raising the gate makes Auto a no-op even
   more often.

3. **Per-distance gating**: regressions cluster at d=0.5 to d=3.0
   on the mid class. A `d >= 5` only gate would close some cells
   but `1418519 d=2.0` still fails.

None of these are cheap. The W38-2 ~11 ms/photo expected saving
turned into a measured ~2 ms median because the gate-firing rate on
this wider corpus is low (15 of 60 cells = 25 %) and the variance
across cells is large (-72 ms to +74 ms).

